<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry V of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a>–<a href="/years/1422">1422</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Henry V, Henry of Monmouth · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lancaster">House of Lancaster</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry V was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Lord of Ireland and Duke of Aquitaine from 1413 until his death in 1422. The eldest son of Henry IV, he gained military experience in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> against the Glyndŵr rebellion and fought at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> when only 16. In 1415 he renewed the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, besieged Harfleur and won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a>, then from 1417 set out to conquer Normandy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> of 1420 made him regent and heir to Charles VI, but Henry died in August 1422, seven weeks before Charles, and was succeeded by the infant Henry VI.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-v-of-england.jpg" alt="Henry V of England" width="330" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_School,_16th_century_-_Henry_V_(1387-1422)_-_RCIN_403443_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born in the gatehouse tower of Monmouth Castle, the eldest son of Henry of Bolingbroke and Mary de Bohun. His birth was not formally recorded, because he stood far from the succession, and its date was disputed for a time; modern scholarship finds 16 September 1386 most likely. His formal schooling seems to have begun around the age of eight. He became a relatively bookish king, with good <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and fluent French, and the first English monarch literate in English.</p><p>At the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> on 21 July 1403, aged sixteen, he was struck in the face by an arrow that lodged six inches into his skull. The royal physician John Bradmore dressed the wound with honey and designed a screw-tool to extract the arrowhead, flushing the wound with white wine. The operation succeeded but may have left permanent scars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry succeeded on 20 March 1413 and was crowned on 9 April in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. He had set his sights on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> early, citing old commercial grievances, French help for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> and the dynastic claim first pursued by Edward III, while France was split between Armagnac and Burgundian factions. In 1415 he took Harfleur and marched towards Calais, and in 1417 he landed near Touques with around 10,000 men to occupy Normandy town by town, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falaise">Falaise</a>.</p><p>On 10 September 1419 Dauphinist partisans murdered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a> at Montereau, driving his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> into an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a>, signed on 21 May 1420, disinherited the Dauphin and made Henry heir and regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with his marriage to Catherine of Valois following on 2 June. The Dauphinist lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> never accepted it. At home Henry worked closely with Parliament and suppressed Lollardy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry fell ill in June 1422, probably with dysentery, and died at Vincennes in the early hours of 31 August, aged thirty-five. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> deserted the English at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1435">Congress of Arras</a> in 1435 the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a> collapsed, and only Calais remained English until 1558.</p><p>Contemporary views divided. English writers such as John Lydgate praised a great conqueror, while Burgundian chroniclers admired his discipline and the chronicler Georges Chastellain portrayed a tyrant driven by vainglory. Modern scholars disagree too, some calling him a warmonger whose ambition bankrupted his realm, others ranking him among the finest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s kings. Shakespeare's Henriad shaped his popular image, although there is little contemporary evidence for the riotous youth of Prince Hal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry V of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It recounts the Battle of Agincourt of 1415, in which the English army led by <b>Henry V of England</b> defeated the army of the French king Charles VI, in what is now the Pas-de-Calais region of France.</blockquote><a href="/songs/agincourt-carol">Agincourt Carol</a></li><li><blockquote>It has also been linked with the earlier, shorter siege of about 1407 to 1409, between the forces of Owain Glyndŵr and the future <b>Henry V of England</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry V of England</b> rode into Paris alongside Charles VI of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a six-month siege that left thousands starving, Rouen yielded to <b>Henry V of England</b>, completing his conquest of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
